  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Care is Essential Infrastructure</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Zimbabwe has been implementing a Women&apos;s Economic Empowerment and Care (WE -Care) project which aims to tackle the unequal distribution of unpaid care and domestic work (UCDW) that negatively affect women and girls&apos; wellbeing and time use, to enable women to have more choice over how they spend their time, greater opportunity to claim their rights and engage in social, personal, economic and political activity. Despite UCDW being the fuel that runs homes and economies across the globe, it remains invisible and undervalued; little or no investment is made by individuals, governments, or the private sector to address it. In addition, in recent years heavy and unequal unpaid care, and domestic work (UCDW) has been increasingly recognised as a barrier to women&apos;s empowerment. The project work in Zimbabwe combines investment in time and labour -saving infrastructure, alongside actions that address social norms which inhibit women and girls and men and boys from redistributing care work at both household and community level, as well as increasing the voice and representation of carers in the recognition of care work as a development and economic issue. Despite these sterling efforts, Government agencies including policy makers and the Private Sector do not perceive, see the provision of care infrastructure as a policy and economic development issue worthy of increased public and private investment. The documentation of the case study will go a long way in outlining the status quo and be a useful tool for advocacy and influencing for increased public and private sector investment in care infrastructure; The research will be grounded in exploring how care infrastructure is understood across civil society groups and government actors. In particular, the research will focus on women&apos;s rights organization, women informal workers, and women political and social leaders. This may allow the research to understand the links between social protections and strengthening care infrastruc</narrative>
  </description>
